{"id":"a3cbb3dd-fba7-4080-bf43-97accba5417b","object":"chat.completion","created":1785009028,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"import heapq\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    # Validate required fields\\n    if 'nodes' not in case or 'utility_threshold' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing required fields: nodes or utility_threshold\\\")\\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\\n    nodes_list = case['nodes']\\n    if not isinstance(nodes_list, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"nodes must be a list\\\")\\n    \\n    # Build node dictionary and children mapping\\n    nodes = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    depth = {}\\n    for node in nodes_list:\\n        if any(k not in node for k in ('id','parent','depth','removable','utility')):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Node missing required field\\\")\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        nodes[nid] = node\\n        depth[nid] = node['depth']\\n        parent = node['parent']\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n        # ensure each node has a children list even if no children\\n        children.setdefault(nid, [])\\n    \\n    # Sets for tracking\\n    removed = set()\\n    queued = set()\\n    heap = []\\n    \\n    # Initialize heap with descending depth, then ascending id\\n    for nid in nodes:\\n        key = (-depth[nid], nid)\\n        heapq.heappush(heap, key)\\n        queued.add(nid)\\n    \\n    visit_order = []\\n    \\n    while heap:\\n        _, current = heapq.heappop(heap)\\n        if current not in queued:\\n            continue  # duplicate popped\\n        queued.remove(current)\\n        visit_order.append(current)\\n        if current in removed:\\n            continue\\n        node = nodes[current]\\n        # check if leaf: no children remaining\\n        if len(children.get(current, [])) > 0:\\n            continue  # not a leaf, cannot remove\\n        if node['removable'] and node['utility'] <= threshold:\\n            # remove this node\\n            removed.add(current)\\n            parent_id = node['parent']\\n            if parent_id is not None:\\n                # remove from parent's children list\\n                if current in children.get(parent_id, []):\\n                    children[parent_id].remove(current)\\n                # enqueue parent if not already queued and not removed\\n                if parent_id not in removed and parent_id not in queued:\\n                    queued.add(parent_id)\\n                    heapq.heappush(heap, (-depth[parent_id], parent_id))\\n    \\n    # Build retained list (any order, sorted for stability)\\n    retained = sorted([nid for nid in nodes if nid not in removed])\\n    return {\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained,\\n        \\\"visit_order\\\": visit_order\\n    }\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) according to the specification. The task is about a tree reduction queue: given a tree with nodes having id, parent, depth, removable, utility, and a utility_threshold. We need to repeatedly remove removable leaf nodes whose utility <= threshold. After removal, the parent (if exists) becomes a leaf candidate again and should be enqueued for reconsideration. The queue is initialized in descending depth and stable node-ID order. We must maintain a visit order of nodes considered (i.e., processed). Return retained_node_ids (IDs of nodes that remain after removals) and visit_order (order in which nodes were considered). The process: start with all nodes? Actually, we need to process nodes in some order: initialize a queue with nodes in descending depth and stable node-ID order. Then process each node from the queue: if it is still present (not removed), and it is a leaf (i.e., no children remaining), and removable, and utility <= threshold, then remove it. Then after removal, its parent (if not null) becomes a leaf possibly, so we need to enqueue the parent for reconsideration. The visit order should record each node we consider (i.e., pop from queue). If we skip due to node already removed, we still record it? The spec says \"Visit order\" likely includes all nodes considered. \"retained_node_ids\" are ids of nodes that remain after all removals.\n\nWe need to be careful: after removing a node, its parent might now be a leaf and could be removable. But note that the parent might have been processed earlier in the queue (since we start with descending depth, deeper nodes first). After removal, the parent's depth is smaller, so it might be later in the original queue order. However, we enqueue it for reconsideration. To avoid revisiting invalidated nodes (no_revisit rule), we should not revisit nodes that were already processed and not removed? Actually, we need to consider the parent again after removal. So we add it to the queue again. The queue order should be maintained? The spec says \"After removal enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration.\" It doesn't specify priority. But we should probably maintain the queue order: maybe we should insert it in appropriate order based on descending depth and stable node-ID? Or just append to end? The spec says \"Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order.\" For subsequent enqueues, we should maintain that order? The \"queue_order\" contract: the queue is always in descending depth and stable node-ID. So when we add a parent, we need to insert it at the correct position to maintain that order. Since we are processing nodes in that order, we can use a priority queue or just a list and insert appropriately. But we also need to avoid duplicate entries: we should not enqueue a node that is already in the queue? However, we might re-enqueue a node that was already processed and removed? No, only parent that is retained (still present). It might have been processed earlier if its depth was larger? Actually, parent depth is smaller, so it would have been later in the original order. If it was already processed (popped from queue), we should not process it again if it was not removed? But we need to reconsider it after child removal. So we need to allow re-processing. The spec says \"no_invalidated_revisit\" probably means we should not revisit nodes that have been invalidated by previous removals (i.e., nodes that are no longer leaves). But we are explicitly enqueuing the parent because it becomes a leaf again. That is allowed. However, we must avoid enqueuing it if it is already in the queue? That might cause duplicate processing. Better to maintain a set of queued nodes or check if already in queue before adding. Alternatively, we can maintain a priority queue and when we pop, we check if node is still existing and valid leaf.\n\nSimplify: Use a list as queue, initialized sorted descending depth and stable node-ID (using id string for tie-breaking). Then process while queue not empty: pop first element. Record it in visit_order. If node already removed (in a set of removed ids), skip. Else, check if it is a leaf: no children remaining. Maintain children mapping initially from parent to list of children. When we remove a child, we remove it from parent's children list. So leaf condition: parent mapping for node has no children (or we can check if any node has this node as parent and is not removed). Then, if node is removable and utility <= threshold, then remove it: add to removed set, and also remove from parent's children list. Then, if parent is not null, enqueue parent for reconsideration: add parent id to queue, maintaining order (insert at correct position to keep descending depth). But we must not enqueue if parent already in queue? To avoid infinite loops, we should only enqueue if parent is not already in queue and is not removed. Since we might have multiple children removed, parent could be enqueued multiple times. But we can handle by checking if parent is already in queue set. We'll maintain a set of queued ids. When we add a node to queue, add to set; when we pop, remove from set. This ensures only one instance. Alternatively, we can just add and later skip duplicates, but that could cause infinite loop if we keep re-adding. So better to have set.\n\nInitialize mapping: nodes_dict by id. Build children list for each parent. Also maintain depth map. Queue initial: list of all node ids sorted by depth descending, then by id stable (lexicographic). Use that order. Then process.\n\nWe need to return retained_node_ids: all ids of nodes not removed. And visit_order: list of ids in order we popped them (including skipped removed ones? The spec: \"Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\" Visit order likely includes every node we considered, even if already removed. Because we \"visit\" it when we pop from queue. So record all. But we skip processing if already removed? Yes, but still record. That seems plausible.\n\nWe must be careful about parent enqueue: after removal, parent might become leaf. But we need to reconsider it. However, if parent is already in queue, but not yet processed, we might still need to reconsider? Actually, the original queue order might process parent later at its original depth. But after child removal, parent's depth hasn't changed. However, the parent was already queued with depth d. When we enqueue it again, it's the same depth. But if we only add if not already queued, we avoid duplicate. But is it correct? Suppose parent is already in queue with depth d. When we later pop it, it will be processed. At that time, it might still be a leaf (if no other children) and if removable etc., it will be removed. But what if the child removal happens after parent has already been popped? That's the scenario: parent was already processed earlier (since depth smaller, later in queue, so depth smaller = later? Actually descending depth: deeper nodes first. So parent depth smaller, so parent comes later in queue. So if we process a child (deeper) first, the parent hasn't been popped yet. So when we enqueue parent, it might already be in queue. But we don't want to process it twice. So we should not enqueue if already in queue. However, is it possible that parent was already popped earlier? Only if there is another child at same depth? No, parent depth smaller, so it will be popped after all deeper nodes. So parent won't have been popped before processing deepest children. So it's safe to not enqueue if already queued. But consider case where multiple children of same parent are processed. When first child removed, we enqueue parent (if not already queued). Then second child removed, parent is already queued, so we skip. That's fine. So we only enqueue if not already in queue set.\n\nBut what about the case where parent was already popped and processed earlier? Could that happen? If parent has no children initially? Then it is a leaf. If it's removable and utility <= threshold, it would be removed during its turn. That's fine. If it is not removed, then it remains. Later, if a child appears? No, children only exist if they were there originally. So parent won't become leaf again after being processed because it was already a leaf? Actually, a non-leaf node that is processed early? But non-leaf nodes are not leaves, so they won't be removable. They will be skipped (not removed). Then later, children may be removed, making parent a leaf. But parent was already processed and skipped. So it needs to be reconsidered. That's the case: parent is not a leaf initially, so when it's popped, we check leaf condition: it has children, so not leaf, so it remains. We move on. Later, after its children are removed, it becomes a leaf. So we need to enqueue it again. But parent was already popped and removed from queue set. So it's not in queue set. So we can enqueue it again. That's correct. So our approach of enqueuing only if not already in queue set works, because when parent was popped, it was removed from set. So when we later add it, it will be added.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Parse case: case is the payload from fixture row. It has \"nodes\" (list) and \"utility_threshold\" (float), and maybe \"_case_nonce\" (ignore). Also each node has \"id\", \"parent\" (string or null), \"depth\" (int), \"removable\" (bool), \"utility\" (float). Validate: all required fields exist. If not, raise ValueError.\n\n2. Build dictionaries:\n   - nodes: dict id -> node object.\n   - children: dict id -> list of child ids (initially empty). For each node, if parent is not null, add node id to children[parent].\n   - depth: dict id -> depth.\n   - removed: set().\n   - queued: set().\n\n3. Initialize queue: list of all node ids sorted by depth descending (higher depth first), and for equal depth, by id lexicographic (stable). Python's sort is stable, so we can sort by (-depth, id). This gives descending depth, then id ascending (since we want stable, id order is fine). We'll store as list.\n\n4. Add all ids to queued set.\n\n5. Visit order list = [].\n\n6. While queue:\n   - id = queue.pop(0) (or use collections.deque for efficiency, but fine with list).\n   - remove id from queued set.\n   - visit_order.append(id).\n   - if id in removed: continue (skip).\n   - node = nodes[id]\n   - Check leaf: len(children.get(id, [])) == 0 (children list after removals? Children list is built initially and we will remove child ids from parent's children list when child is removed. So we need to maintain children dict mutable: for each parent, we have list of child ids. When we remove a child, we remove that child from parent's list. So we can do: if parent_id is not None: children[parent_id].remove(id). So leaf condition: children.get(id, []) must be empty.\n   - if leaf and node['removable'] and node['utility'] <= threshold:\n        - removed.add(id)\n        - If node['parent'] is not None:\n            parent_id = node['parent']\n            # Remove this node from