ntain queued set to prevent duplicate. When we pop from heapq, we check if id is still in queued set? Actually, we push with id, and we have set. When we pop, we can verify that id is still in queued (i.e., not already removed). But if duplicate is present, we might pop same id twice. To avoid that, we can check if id is removed or not in queued (meaning already processed). But if a duplicate exists, both will have same id. So after first pop, we remove from queued set. When second pop comes, we check: id not in queued, so skip. That works. So we can use heapq. However, heapq does not support custom comparator; we need to use (key, id). Since depth is descending, we use negative depth. But also tie-break on id ascending. So key = (-depth, id). Then heapq.heappop gives smallest key, which means highest depth first (since -depth more negative), and for same depth, smallest id first. That's exactly descending depth then ascending id. Good.\n\nImplementation with heapq:\n\nimport heapq\n\nInitialize heap = []\nFor each node id, push (-depth, id, id) (third id to differentiate if depths equal? Actually tuple comparison uses second id; third is ignored but tuple length must match when popping? We'll just store (-depth, id). Then push. Also add to queued set.\n\nThen while heap:\n   key, current_id = heapq.heappop(heap)\n   if current_id not in queued: continue (already popped before)\n   queued.remove(current_id)\n   visit_order.append(current_id)\n   if current_id in removed: skip processing.\n   else process leaf removal as above.\n   If removal occurs and parent enqueued:\n        if parent not in removed and parent not in queued:\n            queued.add(parent)\n            heapq.heappush(heap, (-depth[parent], parent))\n\nThis works.\n\nImportant: When we remove a node, we must update children of its parent. So we need children dict as list of child ids (mutable). Also for leaf check: we check children[current_id] length (empty). Initially, children dict includes all